\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $K_N$ denote the complete simple graph on $N$ vertices. Define $R(5,5)$ to be the least positive integer $N$ such that every assignment of one of two colors, red and blue, to each edge of $K_N$ contains five vertices all of whose ten connecting edges have the same color. Equivalently, every simple graph on $N$ vertices contains either a five-vertex clique or a five-vertex independent set.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine the exact integer $R(5,5)$. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.15709}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
What is the smallest number of vertices that forces a monochromatic complete graph on five vertices in every two-coloring?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1236 (COMB-013). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Vigleik Angeltveit and Brendan D. McKay, \emph{$R(5,5)\leq46$}, arXiv:2409.15709v2 (2025), Section 1. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.15709}.
\end{description}
